# The UI contract comes first: the model, then the screen, then the code

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: leaf (presentation, tests, the modeling thread), reached from `process-check-rules-skills.mdc`; its procedure is the skill `.agents/skills/adding-ui-anchors/SKILL.md`.

## Use This When

- A view gains, renames or loses an anchor, a code, a state, a choice value, a control, a list, a dialog or a screen.
- A file under `contract/` or `tests/e2e/scenarios/` changes, an e2e test names the contract, or a test or an agent cannot find an element.

## Stop Here If

- `bun run contract:check`, `contract:check:sources`, `contract:check:consumers`, `e2e:coverage` and `contract:coverage:check` are green, `bun run contract:coverage --unmarked` lists no unmarked control, and the scenario that reaches the element has its test.

## Descend To

- The steps, the patterns of our kit and views, the checklists and the templates: `.agents/skills/adding-ui-anchors/SKILL.md` and its `references/`. The closed lists: `contract/vocabulary.md`, never copied. Every key and rule of the contract, each written once: `contract/REFERENCE.md`, generated. The folder for people: `contract/README.md`.
- Visible text and `aria-label` keys: `ui-i18n-direct-keys.mdc`. The controls: `ui-primitive-hygiene.mdc`.

## Core rule

- **Model first.** Completeness comes from `contract/domain/model.schema.json`: a screen unfolds its entities, the anchors bind their fields and actions, and the DOM, the tests and the scenarios are checked back against the contract. A change starts in the model, then the screen file, then the scenario, then the code and the tests; an anchor, a code, a state or a route never appears first in JSX or in a test.
- **Who decides.** The developer programs the architecture in the contract, apart from the implementation: the model, the screens with their flows, the platform, the activities and the scenarios, every architectural decision taken there. The agent brings the patterns that make the contract good, proposed for the developer's review with their trade-offs, and builds the implementation from the contract the developer approved. The code takes no architectural decision of its own: a question the code raises goes back to the contract and its review.
- **Two layers, three kinds of commit.** The business layer is the entry point of every business feature: `contract/domain/`, the scenarios of `tests/e2e/scenarios/` and the content tables of `contract/vocabulary.md`. The meta layer is the engine that keeps the contract working: `@workspace/ui-contract`, the generated `contract/schemas/` and `contract/REFERENCE.md`, the grammar of `contract/vocabulary.md`, `contract/README.md`, the rules and skills about the contract and the contract steps of the pre-commit hook. An app is one consumer of the engine: its code and its tests are the implementation. On a branch a commit holds one of the three, never two, and a feature that needs a new facet lands the meta change first, then commits the business change that uses it (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-layers-and-commits`). The contract version is said only in `contract/domain/platform.yaml`.
- **Branches**, by `branch-reuse-first.mdc`, whose example this is. The meta layer's area is the contract engine, and its branch is `feat/contract-first-pass`: a new round first merges the latest `main` into it, then takes its meta commits, and the branch lands on `main` as a squash of its own after the developer's go on its hand-over, which names the meta commits by hash and the business change that will use them, with the engine's specs showing the facet in use. The go covers exactly those commits: one round is open at a time, and a meta commit added after the hand-over, another feature's included, needs a new hand-over of the whole round before the squash. A change that hand-over's review brings is, as in the business review below, a commit of its own on top, never an amend or a re-cut. A feature's business layer is modelled on the contract trunk, `contract/trunk` (`branch-reuse-first.mdc`), once the meta it needs has landed and the trunk has merged `main`; the feature's own branch takes the card's trunk commits as one business commit, its implementation commits go on top, and it lands on `main` as its own squash. A facet found missing in the middle of a feature takes the same road: its meta goes on `feat/contract-first-pass` and lands, `main` is merged into the trunk and into the feature branch (the contract commits already taken are never rebased), and the business commit that uses it is modelled on the trunk and taken like the first.
- **Review gate.** A change to what the app shows or does runs in two phases. First the contract: the business layer (the model, the screen file with its anchors, facets and flow's states, events, guards and effects, the platform, the activities and the scenarios), on the contract trunk, after the meta layer has landed when a facet is new. A behaviour goes into that structure, never into the prose of a `description`; one the contract cannot say is signalled (`contract-gap-no-emulation.mdc`). The developer models it in a modeling session, which may read the code but writes none of it while it models: the agent proposes the contract with the patterns that make it good and shows `bun run contract:diff main` with the diff, each decision is a commit of its own on top, never an amend or a re-cut, so the developer follows it as its own step, and the card names its trunk commits and the developer's word that its contract is right, which is the OK. The implementation (the code, its tests and the e2e tests, their scaffold included, and the docs that quote the code) starts only after the OK, on the area branch, in commits of its own on top of the card's take, which copies the commits the developer approved and so needs no stop of its own; any session may write it, the modeling session included. The contract and the code are never written at the same time, and the code never before its contract. The OK covers the card's trunk commits, and the one commit it approves in advance: the last business commit of a rename or a removal, written on the area branch, which drops the old names the card listed and nothing else. A contract change found during the implementation goes back to the first phase: asked of the modeling session, committed on the trunk, approved there, and taken as one more commit. A business commit written on an area branch instead, a feature begun there before the trunk or an amendment the developer lets an implementer write there, stops for review: hand the developer the contract diff (`bun run contract:diff main`, the diff of those commits named by hash, the meta landing they build on, the reason for each change, the gaps signalled), ask for the OK on those commits, and wait. A change that review brings, one the developer asks for or one an answer to their question leads to, is a commit of its own on top of the ones under review, never an amend or a re-cut; the OK is then asked on all of them. Any other contract commit made, re-cut, amended or rebased after that OK, even with its files unchanged, and a return to the implementation after other work, stop again, and no commit of the implementation stays on the branch without an OK on the contract commits beneath it. The gate holds whenever the intended behaviour changes or the contract needs a correction or an extension; a fix that brings the code back to what the contract already says, correctly, needs no contract diff and no stop. A screen's transient state comes from its flow run as its machine, never from a component's own flags.
- **The files.** Authored: `contract/vocabulary.md`, `contract/domain/screens/<pathname>.yaml` (`__root.yaml` for the frame), `contract/domain/platform.yaml`, `contract/domain/model.schema.json`, `contract/domain/activities/<id>.yaml`, `contract/domain/terms.yaml` (the project's terms, their forbidden synonyms and the roles; `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-terms`). Generated, never edited: the schemas of `contract/schemas/`, `contract/REFERENCE.md` (`bun run contract:schemas`) and the block of `contract/COVERAGE.md`. A word the vocabulary lacks is added to it in the same change. Code and tests name a role or a route through `CONTRACT_ROLES` and `CONTRACT_ROUTES` (`ui-contract emit terms`), never by hand (ESLint `workspace/contract-terms`). Every description a domain file writes says what its element is for and keeps 200 characters at most (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-check-files`, `contract/README.md`, "Descriptions"). A platform action that sends a mail declares it, and a mail that only tells is a notice (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-check-platform`); every action says under `changes` the reads it changes (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-read-after-an-action`).
- **Grammar.** An anchor (`data-testid`) is 2 to 4 dot-separated kebab-case segments, a screen name first and a kind last (`user.sign-in.email-input`); it says what the element is for, never its text, colour, position or an entity's id (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-anchor-grammar`).
- **One literal per rendered name**, declared once in one screen file and written once in the sources; no anchor equals another plus a derived suffix, and an anchor is never built at runtime (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-anchor-grammar`).
- **Derived names only through `testIdFor`.** A composite takes one `testId` and builds its parts with `testIdFor(testId, '<suffix>')`, the suffix from the vocabulary and in the base's `derived` (`contract/REFERENCE.md#screen-anchors-name-derived`); a part with a screen-level name of its own is a named prop (`navigationTestId`).
- **Placement.** On the semantic element whose role the kind maps to, never on a wrapper, a fragment, a portal or a backdrop (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-marking-a-page`).
- **What needs one.** A container that scopes a test, every entry of a list, every control a test or an agent drives, a value a test reads, every state and message; a control that is not a test target carries a coverage-ignore marker, never a made-up anchor, and an icon-only control also carries an `aria-label` (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-marking-a-page`).
- **Codes, states and values, never wording.** `data-code`, `data-state` and `data-value` say what varies, and a behaviour that varies is a code or a state, never a second anchor (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-attributes`). An error's `fields` name the field each of its codes concerns (`contract/REFERENCE.md#screen-anchors-name-fields`); a message whose codes more than one source may raise says `ranked: true` (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-notices`). A field that holds a secret says `secret: true` and never travels in an address (`contract/REFERENCE.md#screen-anchors-name-secret`). The loader derives whether a field holds input, and a view writes neither that nor a field's state colour, limit or type: the kit takes them from the generated field facts (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-field-holds-input`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-field-states-carried`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-check-sources`). Every leave that would lose unsaved input is declared in `platform.yaml` under `unsavedInput`, and a control that moves to another screen asks before its event (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-leave-questions`).
- **Repeated items** share one static anchor with `data-id`; an entry that differs or changes in place carries a row state. **A composite mounted on its own is not a singleton**: the shell's instance owns the reserved anchor, every other mount passes its own `testId`, and a feature never renders a reserved anchor (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-marking-a-page`).
- **Scenarios** are Markdown files, `tests/e2e/scenarios/<entity>/<layer>/[<area>/]<slug>.md` beside `tests/e2e/src/<same path>.e2e.spec.ts`, whose brackets name what the contract declares, and the contract's feedback loop: a scenario that cannot be written from the contract alone names a gap to fix in the contract files, never one to work around in a test; a gap the contract cannot close in the change is signalled, never emulated (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-scenario-brackets`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-scenario-tests`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-contract-gaps`, `contract-gap-no-emulation.mdc`).
- **Routes.** A binding is a TanStack Router route and a state address, whose `roles` are the roles it serves; the router serves it only to an account that holds one of them and opens the not-found screen at the same address for any other (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-roles`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-route-bindings`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-route-example`). `apps/web/src/platform/routes-contract.spec.ts` and `routes-router.spec.ts` hold the module routes and the real router to it, and `routes-examples.spec.ts` its example link (`bun run contract:routes`) and the route tree.
- **Flows.** A screen's `flow` is an XState v5 machine config in the subset `setup({ guards, actions, actors }).createMachine(flow)` takes as written; the route owns the addressable state, the machine the transient one, and the app builds its machine for a screen from the flow and holds it to it with the engine's comparison (`apps/web/src/platform/machines.spec.ts`). A control whose anchor is an event is enabled exactly in the states that handle it, read from the machine, never from a flag, and the app reads what a state renders from its machine (`renderedAt`, `isRendered`). The read after a success is mandatory. A screen file may write its flow shorter (`bun run contract:flow <screen>` prints the full config) and **leaves out what the contract says elsewhere**: what an action fails with, a button's params, an error's codes and fields, the failure and the outcome of a request, the region of a button, the composites of the platform and the descriptions the engine says itself. The facts of each are in the reference: states (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-flow-states`), events and guards (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-flow-events`), effects (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-flow-effects`), invoke (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-flow-invoke`), the shorter forms (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-flow-shorter-forms`), the walk (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-flow-run`), what a state renders (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-renders`), the reads (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-list-reads`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-read-after-an-action`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-paged-list`), what a screen leaves out (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-what-stays`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-failure-codes`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-button-params`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-error-codes-and-fields`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-failure-show`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-request-outcome`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-button-region`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-composite-of-the-platform`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-engine-descriptions`).
- **Activities.** A task across screens is an activity, `contract/domain/activities/<id>.yaml`: an XState machine config whose states are visits to screens, which a scenario that walks it names in its `activity` (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-activities`).
- **The checks**, in `bun run check` and the pre-commit hook: `contract:check`, `contract:check:sources` (the JSX of `apps/web/src` and `packages/ui/src`) and `contract:check:consumers` (the literals of `tests/e2e/src`); a commit on `main` and `bun run check`, which checks the tip of a branch before it lands, also check that the contract is at the version the engine reads (`contract:check --current`), and take the full check, `e2e:coverage` and `contract:coverage:check` included, so nothing lands unrendered, half-built, untested or with its coverage block behind. On a branch the hook lets the contract run ahead of the app, so a rename or a removal goes in three commits: the business commit adds the new name and keeps the old one, the implementation switches to it, a last business commit on the area branch drops the old one, approved with the first (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-checks`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-ahead-mode`). The hooks hold `contract/trunk`, a take and a landing to their forms (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-trunk-commits`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-take-form`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-landing-form`; `scripts/contract-trunk.ts`, `scripts/contract-take.ts`). What the checks read is in `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-check-files`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-check-sources` and `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-check-consumers`. A control off the coverage carries `// ui-contract-ignore[-self]: <reason>` or `{/* ui-contract-ignore[-self]: <reason> */}` right before its element.
- **The manifest** is built only after the check passes and served by every stand at `/ui-manifest/manifest.json` (`PERCH_UI_MANIFEST=0` turns it off); development and e2e builds add `window.__perch.ui.anchors()` (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-manifest`, `contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-page-helper`). A contract change hands over `bun run contract:diff main` with the reason for each rename or removal (`contract/REFERENCE.md#rule-hand-over`).
